<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.13.3: Writing Equations that Represent a Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01961</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.13.3: Writing Equations that Represent a Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cd4d933d-2a81-476a-bcbc-25ff01ad74f4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (10 minutes)</title>
<para>Earlier, students were given quadratic equations and asked to reason about the graphs that represent them. Here, they reason the other way around: they write equations that can be represented by given graphs. To do so, students need to apply their insights about the structure of quadratic expressions in different forms and their connections to the graphs.</para>
<para>To help students write and check their equations, at least three points on each graph are labeled with their coordinates. In some cases, it is easier to write an equation in factored form. In other cases, an equation in standard form can be more easily derived. Identify students who are attentive to the information given in the graph and use it strategically. Invite them to share during discussion.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Provide access to graphing technology.</para>
<para>The activity allows for instant feedback to students (if an equation is incorrect, the graph that results will not match the given graph). Because the equations increase in complexity as students move from Graph A to Graph J, students may find that their equations are off but not know what the issue is or how to correct them. Consider arranging students in groups of two so that they can discuss how to write the more-challenging equations.</para>
<!--BEGIN ELL AND SWD GRAY BOX -->
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Conversing<title>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Conversing</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>Use this routine to help students describe their strategies for writing equations. Arrange students in groups of two. Invite Partner A to begin with this sentence frame: “Equation _____ matches with graph _____, because . . . .” Invite the listener, Partner B, to press for additional details referring to specific features of the graphs such as intercepts, vertex, and direction of opening. Students should switch roles for each equation. This will help students justify how features of quadratic graphs can be used to write equations.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Support sense-making; Cultivate conversation</para>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for Students with Disabilities<title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title></para>
    <para>Engagement: Provide Access by Recruiting Interest<title>Engagement: Provide Access by Recruiting Interest</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>Leverage choice around perceived challenge. Invite students to find functions for at least four or five of the graphs. Be sure to let students know that the equations increase in complexity as students move from Graph A to Graph J.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Supports accessibility for: Organization; Attention; Social-emotional skills</para>
  </div>
</div>
<!--END ELL AND SWD GRAY BOX -->
<title>Student Activity </title>
<para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Identify the function that matches each graph. Make sure your graph goes through all three points shown!</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the function of Graph A.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Coordinate plane, graph of a quadratic function, point at negative 1 comma 1, minimum at 0 comma 0, point at 1 comma 1.&quot; height=&quot;197&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6ed1fd586c488b553eeaae6bd68f59a4c5c83160"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=x^2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the function of Graph B.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Coordinate plane, graph of a quadratic function, point at negative 3 comma 0, minimum at 0 comma negative 9, point at 3 comma 0.&quot; height=&quot;197&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9689c8cd0315072ca765e930be4ccc3bcc15f53a"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=x^2-9\) or \(y=(x-3)(x+3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the function of Graph C.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Coordinate plane, graph of a quadratic function, point at negative 2 comma 0, minimum at 0 point 5 comma negative 6 point 2 5, point at 3 comma 0.&quot; height=&quot;197&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/287c43c1298c5030965339438411a3a651a2a2e3"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=(x+2)(x-3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the function of Graph D.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Coordinate plane, graph of a quadratic function, point at negative 6 comma 0, minimum at negative 3 comma negative 9, point at 0 comma 0.&quot; height=&quot;197&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8fc03a70c381320a87af48345282d2d405377d21"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=x(x+6)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the function of Graph E.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="&quot;Coordinate plane, graph of a quadratic function, point at negative 2 comma 0, maximum at negative 0 comma 4, point at 2 comma 0.&quot; height=&quot;197&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4b744ac2506e4dbff39a59ac8059af2d5d1c5a8d"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=-x^2+4\) or \(y=(-x+2)(x+2)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the function of Graph F.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="&quot;Coordinate plane, graph of a quadratic function, points at 0 comma 4, and 4 comma 4,&amp;nbsp;minimum at 2 comma 0.&quot; height=&quot;197&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/36397610098764ae15f839bb1560b1db1385d23b"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=(x-2)(x-2)\) or \(y=x^2-4x+4\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the function of Graph G.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="&quot;Coordinate plane, graph of a quadratic function, point at negative 1 comma 0, maximum at 0 comma 3, point at 3 comma 0.&quot; height=&quot;197&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dfd9304ca8fc0773acec92954f8e4be52f8f457e"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=(-x-1)(x-3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the function of Graph H.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="&quot;Coordinate plane, graph of a quadratic function, opens up, point at 0 comma 5, point at 1 comma 0, point at 5 comma 0.&quot; height=&quot;197&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7bed3e1e35db1c8c15501c9dae2b4aab304a735e"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=(x-1)(x-5)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the function of Graph I.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="&quot;Coordinate plane, graph of a quadratic function, point at negative 1 comma 2, minimum at 0 comma 0, point at 1 comma 2.&quot; height=&quot;197&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/befe24d1f7cbcec510715bab4fc8be493befce13"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=2x^2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the function of Graph J.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Parabola. Opens down. Vertex at origin. Points on parabola include: -4 comma -4 and 4 comma -4.&quot; height=&quot;197&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7504df963257e91177a9d9f6c88a29b3727466ad"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=-0.25x^2\)</para>
<title>Video: Writing Equations that Represent a Quadratic Graph</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about writing equations that represent a quadratic graph.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <iframe><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/296f3aec8fb544a526585ba8f6a0ae3cdcf31337">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d9c5f1c9ec2826f1f07dd571b946edd640fe5491" srclang="en_us">https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/296f3aec8fb544a526585ba8f6a0ae3cdcf31337
    </track></source></video></iframe>
</div>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Select students to share their strategies for writing equations. Highlight explanations such as:</para>
<list>
  <item> When the graph opens up, the \(x^2\) term in standard form has a positive coefficient. When it opens down, the \(x^2\) term has a negative coefficient. </item>
  <item> When two \(x\)-intercepts are known, we can write the equations in factored form. </item>
  <item> When the \(y\)-intercept is not at \((0,0)\) and the graph is centered on the \(y\)-axis, the equation in standard form will have the form of \(ax^2+c\) (no linear term) and \((0,c)\) is the \(y\)-intercept. (A linear term would have shifted the graph horizontally as well as vertically so it would not be centered on the \(y\)-axis.) </item>
  <item> When \((0,0)\) is the only \(x\)-intercept, the \(y\)-intercept, and the vertex, the equation in standard form has only the squared term \((ax^2)\). If there was a linear term, the graph would not be centered on the \(y\)-axis. If there was a constant term, the graph would move up or down and either cross the \(x\)-axis in two places or not cross it at all (that is, there would be two \(x\)-intercepts or none). </item>
</list>
<para>Consider discussing Graph F, as it is unlike most graphs students have seen so far (it has only one \(x\)-intercept at \((2,0)\) and one \(y\)-intercept that is not at the origin). Here a couple of approaches:</para>
<para>Help students see that the factored form is still useful here. Students may guess that (\(x-2)\) is one of the factors (because multiplying any number by \(2-2\) would give an output of 0) but may be unsure about the second factor. Point out that the second factor is also \((x-2)\) because there is no other \(x\)-intercept. Expanding \((x-2)(x-2)\) into standard form gives \(x^2-4x+4\), which allows us to see that the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,4)\).</para>
<para>If we shift this graph down 4 units, it will intercept the \(x\)-axis at 0 and 4 and its equation would be \(y=x(x-4)\) or \(y=x^2-4x\) (per what we learned earlier). To arrive at the Graph F, we would have to move it back up 4, making the equation \(x^2-4x+4\).</para>
<title>7.13.3: Self Check</title>
<!--BEGIN SELF CHECK INTRO BEFORE Tables -->
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which equation describes the graph below?</para>
<para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 12 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 4 AND 3."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/fd9f826367e7f9ba7a5a7cba388f5b67f6852384"/></media></figure></para>
<!--SELF CHECK table-->
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(x) = (x + 3)(x - 4)\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The signs of the constants in the factors should be opposite signs as the \(x\)-intercepts. The answer is \(f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 4)\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 4)\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>That’s correct! Check yourself: The \(x\)-intercepts are at 3 and -4 so the factors are \((x - 3)\) and \((x + 4)\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(x) = x + 12\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Factored form is based on the \(x\)-intercepts, not the vertex. The answer is \(f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 4)\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(x) = x(x + 3)\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The \(x\)-intercepts of this function are 0 and -3. \(x=0\) is not an \(x\)-intercept. Watch the signs when writing the factors from the intercepts. The answer is \(f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 4)\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>7.13.3: Additional Resources</title>
<emphasis><emphasis>
    <para>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</para>
  </emphasis></emphasis>
<title>Writing Quadratic Equations from Graphs</title>
<para>Write an equation for the graph below:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 8 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 2 AND 4."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c055716b4854e25b101b61523b81193fa53c6497"/></media></figure></para>
<para>First, locate the \(x\)-intercepts at \((-2,0)\) and \((4,0)\).</para>
<para>The factors that result in the given zeros are \((x + 2)\) and \((x - 4)\).</para>
<para>Use graphing technology to check your answer.</para>
<title>Try It: Writing Quadratic Equations from Graphs</title>
<para>Write an equation for the graph below:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 12 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 6 AND 2."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/21d2c1a12d77163c8bb135021166ab7f3494cee3"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to write an equation for the function graphed:</para>
<para>First, identify the \(x\)-intercepts: \(x = -6\), \(x = 2.\)</para>
<para>The function in factored form is \(f(x)=(x + 6)(x - 2)\).</para></section>
